# Problem found during pre-deploy cleanup
Goal 23 lowered the caps (board MAX_NODES 600→100, ledger MAX_LEASES 1000→100) as a single constant used by BOTH reads and writes. A live scan showed four boards already above 100 nodes:

- aae7db361c7f/10b90b…: 339 nodes (9 done / 330 open, 20 open roots — unfinished work alone exceeds the cap)
- aae7db361c7f/b40085…: 149 nodes (8 done / 141 open)
- aae7db361c7f/c9df7e…: 104 nodes (21 done / 83 open)
- bd5f642b3583/8d346e… (this session): 314 nodes (285 done / 29 open — shrinks by itself via 24h completed retention)
- Live parallel ledger: 2 leases (waiting_input) — no overflow.

Deploying the naive cap would have made those boards unreadable ("nodes exceed the node limit") and blocked the sessions owning them. Unfinished items must never be discarded to fit a cap — that is the whole point of the queue.

# Fix: the 100 cap binds additions, not reads
## Board (work_plan_tree.py)
- `MAX_NODES = 100` now binds only `add_node` (new root/detail additions).
- New `STORAGE_MAX_NODES = 100_000` structural ceiling for `normalize_document` reads.
- Stored focus lists are bounded by the actual node count, not the addition cap.
- `_depth` cycle guard uses MAX_DEPTH (it was accidentally coupled to MAX_NODES).
- An over-cap board stays fully readable and mutable in place (status updates, removal); only growing it further is refused until completions/retention drain it back under 100.

## Ledger (work_parallel.py)
- Same split: `MAX_LEASES = 100` binds new lease records in reserve; `STORAGE_MAX_LEASES = 100_000` bounds stored-state validation.
- Live ledger (2 leases) is unaffected either way.

# Verification
- New tests: oversized board normalizes, in-place update/remove work, add refused; stored ledger with 151 leases (template rows marked done with idle/cleanup proofs) re-reads fine.
- Live proof: all four oversized live boards (339/149/104/314) normalize successfully under the new code, and the live ledger reads.
- Combined focused suite: 144 passed, 66 subtests.

